    faq=[
        ("What is a good WIP limit?", "There is no universal number. A practical starting point is slightly below your current average WIP, or about one to one and a half items per person in each active stage, then adjust based on cycle time and where work queues."),
        ("How do you calculate a WIP limit?", "There is no exact formula. Count your current WIP per column, note how many people work in each stage, and set the limit a little below today's level. Little's Law (average cycle time = average WIP ÷ average throughput) helps you estimate the effect: if you finish 8 items a week and want an average cycle time of 2 weeks, aim for roughly 16 items in progress across the system."),
        ("What does WIP stand for?", "WIP stands for work in progress (sometimes work in process): items that have been started but not yet finished."),
        ("What happens when a WIP limit is reached?", "Nobody pulls new items into that stage. Team members help finish, review or unblock existing work, starting with the items furthest to the right of the board, until there is space."),
        ("Can a team break a WIP limit?", "Occasionally, for a genuine emergency, but the breach should be visible, recorded and discussed at the next review. A better approach is an expedite lane with its own limit of one. Frequent breaches mean the limit or the process needs to change."),
        ("Should blocked items count towards the WIP limit?", "Yes, in most cases. Counting blocked items creates pressure to resolve blockers and stops a column filling up with stuck work while new items keep arriving."),
        ("Should the Done column have a WIP limit?", "No. Done is the end of the flow, so limiting it would only stop work finishing. Waiting columns inside the workflow, such as Develop: Done or Ready for test, should be counted."),
        ("Do WIP limits slow teams down?", "They can feel slower for the first week or two while people adjust, and throughput may dip briefly. After that, each item usually finishes sooner and delivery becomes more predictable, because work is completed rather than left half-done."),
        ("What is CONWIP?", "CONWIP means constant work in progress. Instead of limiting each column, you cap the total number of items in the whole workflow, starting a new item only when one finishes. It is a simple way to start, but column limits show bottlenecks more clearly."),
        ("Can you set WIP limits per person?", "Yes. A personal limit, such as no more than two items each, helps when individuals are overloaded or take requests from several people. Column limits usually reflect team flow better, so many teams use both. "),
        ("Does Scrum use WIP limits?", "The Scrum Guide does not require them, but the sprint itself limits how much work the team takes on. Many Scrum teams add column limits to their sprint board to finish items earlier and reduce carry-over."),
        ("What is the difference between a WIP limit and capacity?", "Capacity is how much work a team can do in a period, often in hours or items per week. A WIP limit is how many items may be in progress at once. Capacity planning asks how much fits; WIP limits control how much is started. See [capacity planning](/project-management/capacity-planning/)."),
        ("How often should you change WIP limits?", "Review them every two to four weeks, or when the team changes size. Change one or two limits at a time, by one item, so you can see the effect of each change."),
    ],
